there have been many proclaimed game  changers in the medical fight back  against coronavirus this is the latest  the us-made drug molnupiravir was  initially intended to treat the flu it  works by inserting errors into the  genetic code of the virus limiting its  ability to multiply in the body  in practical terms it's the first covet  medicine that can be taken in pill form  at home to help the most vulnerable  avoid severe disease and last month the  uk became the first country to approve  its use i'm a research nurse working on  a new national kobit 19 study calling  you to see if you'd be interested in  taking part researchers at the  university of oxford are testing it in  more than 10 000 volunteers who've  recently contracted covert 19 like 51  year old glynn talling i had to take i  think four in one go  twice a day so with a 12-hour gap  over a period of five days  and nothing special about the medication  literally just normal capsules that you  take with water so all very  straightforward i had no side effects  there are already questions over the  effectiveness of molnupiravir after  clinical trial results led the  manufacturers to reduce the overall  efficacy of the drug from fifty percent  to thirty percent if you look at the  studies generally thirty percent is  actually a very very high level in terms  of the benefits that we see on a  large-scale study  and and that's what we want to replicate  here as well  while nu pirivia is one of several new  home treatments for covid to be tested  in what's being called the panoramic  study they're likely to include the fisa  produced paxlovid it's a combination  therapy based on old hiv treatments that  pfizer claims is up to 90 percent  effective in preventing hospitalizations  in high risk patients  with half a million courses already on  order here in the uk the focus for now  is firmly on molnupiravir if you catch  kovid19 early on and you treat it early  on you might then not be able to develop  any severe symptoms and this means  reduction in  hospital admissions and possibly taking  off some of the pressure on the nhs so  quite an important study i think molnu  pirivia and drugs like it could prove  valuable additions to a fast-growing  medical arsenal jonah al jazeera london
 

